  "slug": "scientific-fair-5429",
  "summary": "New methods reconstruct immune trafficking, metastatic migration histories and lineage-versus-niche effects from static or indirect tumor and tissue measurements.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is recovering dynamic or historical processes from static measurements. CellVADIS applies optimal transport to single-cell snapshots to infer cross-tissue immune cell trafficking. MACH2 enumerates all optimal metastatic migration histories from DNA sequencing data and adds a clone-location parsimony criterion, so the full solution space is characterized rather than a single history. SOUR separates heritable lineage effects from spatial microenvironment effects on tumor cell expression in spatial lineage tracing data. The arginine tumor-immune model, calibrated to mouse data, is the outlier: it is a mechanistic simulation with virtual cohorts predicting immune escape and anti-ARG1/PD-1 synergy, not an inference method for trafficking or lineage. Relative to the previous emphasis on velocity fields and signalling within spatial transcriptomics, the focus moves to inter-tissue trafficking, ambiguity in migration histories, and separating inherited from environmental variation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
